WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:10.510
Without EUV,

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:12.262
will the semiconductor industry
be over?

00:00:12.262 --> 00:00:15.390
This EUV machine costs 181 million USD.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:16.474
Not only is it expensive,

00:00:16.474 --> 00:00:17.892
it also occupies a pivotal position

00:00:17.892 --> 00:00:19.561
in the chip industry chain.

00:00:19.602 --> 00:00:21.688
Under the restrictions of
the U.S. CHIPS Act,

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:24.649
ASML is prohibited from
exporting EUV machines to China.

00:00:24.649 --> 00:00:26.985
Even its EUV machines in Taiwan

00:00:26.985 --> 00:00:29.195
have kill switches installed.

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:30.572
ASML has prepared for the worst,

00:00:30.572 --> 00:00:32.198
making sure that
this technology cannot be stolen

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:34.034
even China invades Taiwan.

00:00:34.075 --> 00:00:37.328
But why exactly is this machine
sought after by the whole world?

00:00:37.328 --> 00:00:40.331
Can't we just build
another company like ASML?

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:45.420
The key technology of making chips

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.422
has been around since 1957.

00:00:47.422 --> 00:00:50.633
It is essentially
stacking materials on silicon wafers.

00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:52.844
Only that each layer of material

00:00:52.844 --> 00:00:55.013
needs to be etched with patterns
through photolithography

00:00:55.013 --> 00:00:56.723
before the next layer stacks on.

00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:58.099
So does the next layer.

00:00:58.099 --> 00:01:00.226
To ensure patterns on chips are correct,

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:01.061
in 1957,

00:01:01.061 --> 00:01:04.064
Lathrop developed
the photolithography process.

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:05.982
Let's do a quick review of this.

00:01:05.982 --> 00:01:08.610
First,
grow the desired material on a wafer.

00:01:08.651 --> 00:01:10.570
Then coat it with photoresist.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.530
The next step—exposure—is the key.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:16.326
Place a photomask
between the UV light source and the wafer.

00:01:16.326 --> 00:01:17.994
The mask is with a carved-out pattern.

00:01:17.994 --> 00:01:19.579
The mask itself blocks UV light.

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:21.498
The UV light passing through the hollow part

00:01:21.498 --> 00:01:24.167
will change the chemical properties
of the photoresist.

00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:25.335
Take negative photoresist for example.

00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:29.297
The photoresist exposed to UV light
becomes insoluble.

00:01:29.297 --> 00:01:32.842
When rinsed with a solvent,
these parts remain.

00:01:32.842 --> 00:01:35.804
The parts that were not exposed to the UV
due to the mask's blocking

00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:37.263
will be rinsed away.

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:40.558
Finally, add hydrofluoric acid or other solvents.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.727
The area on the wafer
not protected by photoresist

00:01:42.727 --> 00:01:45.980
will be etched by the solvent
with desired patterns.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.900
Now, congratulations,
one lithography process is completed.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.194
You have printed
the first layer of a chip.

00:01:51.236 --> 00:01:53.655
Next, just repeat the process
again and again.

00:01:53.655 --> 00:01:56.199
Finally, you should build
3D chips on the wafer.

00:01:56.199 --> 00:01:58.243
When we want to make smaller chips,

00:01:58.243 --> 00:02:02.997
we should try to leave finer patterns
on the photoresist.

00:02:02.997 --> 00:02:03.623
In the past,

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:05.333
simply shrinking the size of the mask

00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:07.460
allows patterns to be smaller and smaller.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.130
But when a mask
shrinks to a certain degree,

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.298
diffraction occurs.

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:13.508
To be precise,

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:16.719
when the slits of a mask are thinner
than half the light's wavelength,

00:02:16.719 --> 00:02:19.347
the light diffracts.

00:02:19.347 --> 00:02:20.849
When passing through such slits,

00:02:20.849 --> 00:02:22.392
the light will not only go straight,

00:02:22.392 --> 00:02:24.018
but also make a turn
at the edge of the slits,

00:02:24.018 --> 00:02:25.436
casting the wrong pattern.

00:02:25.436 --> 00:02:26.521
In other words,

00:02:26.521 --> 00:02:28.314
although we can keep shrinking the mask,

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.483
but to ensure the accuracy of a pattern,

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:31.818
the size of each feature within

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:34.946
cannot be smaller than
half the wavelength of the light.

00:02:34.946 --> 00:02:36.990
For example, in the early 2000s,

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.200
the light source used at that time
was laser.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:40.702
Its wavelength had been reduced

00:02:40.702 --> 00:02:42.579
from krypton fluoride's (KrF2)
248 nanometers

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:45.415
to argon fluoride's (ArF) 193 nanometers.

00:02:45.415 --> 00:02:46.916
This 193-nanometer laser

00:02:46.958 --> 00:02:51.421
is of course inadequate
to the task for current sub-7nm processes.

00:02:51.421 --> 00:02:53.339
To get around this physical limit,

00:02:53.381 --> 00:02:55.717
scientists tried various methods

00:02:55.717 --> 00:02:59.179
such as immersion lithography
and multiple patterning.

00:02:59.179 --> 00:03:02.265
Immersion lithography is to spread
a layer of water or other liquid

00:03:02.265 --> 00:03:04.100
between the light source and the wafer

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.727
to improve the path of light.

00:03:05.727 --> 00:03:06.352
This is a bit like,

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:08.438
when using a high-magnification microscope,

00:03:08.438 --> 00:03:10.356
we would put a drop of immersion oil

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:12.108
between the lens and the sample.

00:03:12.108 --> 00:03:15.695
Multiple patterning is to divide
a pattern into multiple exposures.

00:03:16.112 --> 00:03:16.487
For example,

00:03:16.487 --> 00:03:19.866
if there are 6 lines—1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6,—
to be exposed,

00:03:19.908 --> 00:03:21.701
the lines can be divided into two passes.

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:23.661
The first pass exposes line 1, 3, and 5.

00:03:23.661 --> 00:03:25.330
The second 2, 4, and 6.

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.832
This act of
adding the spacing between lines

00:03:27.832 --> 00:03:31.002
can reduce diffraction as much as possible.

00:03:31.002 --> 00:03:32.503
These methods are collectively called

00:03:32.503 --> 00:03:34.881
resolution enhancement techniques—RET.

00:03:34.881 --> 00:03:38.343
They are meant to achieve higher resolution
at a given wavelength

00:03:38.343 --> 00:03:40.386
by redesigning
lithography processes.

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:42.597
In addition, upon diffraction,

00:03:42.597 --> 00:03:45.516
the UV light deviates more and more

00:03:45.516 --> 00:03:47.227
as the distance between the mask
and the wafer increases.

00:03:47.727 --> 00:03:48.853
In the 1970s,

00:03:48.853 --> 00:03:51.231
some researchers
placed the mask right on the wafer,

00:03:51.231 --> 00:03:53.066
making the mask touch the photoresist

00:03:53.107 --> 00:03:55.693
to minimize the distance between the two.

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:57.612
But with this method,

00:03:57.612 --> 00:03:59.280
when the mask was lifted after exposure,

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.325
it was usually glued with photoresist
and damaged the wafer.

00:04:02.325 --> 00:04:04.202
Eventually, this approach was abandoned.

00:04:04.202 --> 00:04:06.579
Researchers turned to
developing the mask aligner instead.

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:08.456
Using precision equipment,
they can finely adjust

00:04:08.456 --> 00:04:12.335
the distance between the mask and the wafer
and other parameters

00:04:12.335 --> 00:04:13.962
to reduce diffraction.

00:04:13.962 --> 00:04:15.630
With these improvements,

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:18.549
although the light source at that time
was the deep ultraviolet laser—DUV—

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:21.094
with a 193-nanometer wavelength,

00:04:21.135 --> 00:04:21.803
amazingly,

00:04:21.803 --> 00:04:24.597
we still advanced all the way
to the 7nm process node.

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:26.683
This deserves a round of applause.

00:04:26.724 --> 00:04:28.393
But below 7 nanometers,

00:04:28.393 --> 00:04:30.603
DUV lithography ultimately met
its bottleneck.

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:33.898
Scientists still had to
resort to light with shorter wavelengths.

00:04:33.898 --> 00:04:35.358
You might think that

00:04:35.358 --> 00:04:37.986
what's so difficult about that?

00:04:37.986 --> 00:04:39.529
It is incredibly difficult.

00:04:39.529 --> 00:04:42.156
For example,
light must be focused through lenses,

00:04:42.156 --> 00:04:43.700
but ordinary glass lenses

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:45.660
absorb short-wavelength UV light.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:48.204
Ultraviolet light with wavelengths
below 185 nanometers

00:04:48.204 --> 00:04:50.039
can even be absorbed by air.

00:04:50.039 --> 00:04:51.582
Therefore, the entire lithography process

00:04:51.582 --> 00:04:53.376
must be conducted in a vacuum.

00:04:53.376 --> 00:04:54.460
If you dig into these issues,

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.629
you'll find more difficulties.

00:04:56.629 --> 00:04:57.338
Anyway,

00:04:57.338 --> 00:04:59.132
some researchers were finally determined

00:04:59.132 --> 00:05:03.011
to develop a light
with less than one-tenth the wavelength,

00:05:03.052 --> 00:05:07.098
the 13.5-nanometer
extreme ultraviolet light—EUV.

00:05:07.098 --> 00:05:08.933
Thus began a great project

00:05:08.933 --> 00:05:10.727
that would take 30 years,
cost billions of dollars,

00:05:10.727 --> 00:05:15.315
and turned out to be achieved by
only one company in the world.

00:05:18.735 --> 00:05:21.029
In previous episodes, we mentioned that

00:05:21.029 --> 00:05:24.615
the leader of the photolithography
equipment market kept changing.

00:05:24.615 --> 00:05:26.868
Since Lathrop invented this technology,

00:05:26.868 --> 00:05:29.704
From Texas Instruments, PerkinElmer, GCA,

00:05:29.704 --> 00:05:32.373
to Nikon and Canon in the 1980s...

00:05:32.373 --> 00:05:35.335
Successively, they have been
the key manufacturers at different times.

00:05:35.335 --> 00:05:37.712
Eventually, in the 1990s, a crucial period,

00:05:37.712 --> 00:05:40.173
a new challenger, ASML, emerged.

00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:42.133
Nikon, Canon, and ASML

00:05:42.133 --> 00:05:45.094
became the three giants competing
in the lithography equipment market.

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:46.929
However, a decision made by Intel

00:05:46.929 --> 00:05:49.223
altered the competitive landscape.

00:05:49.223 --> 00:05:52.352
At that time, the lab led by Intel
and the US government believed that

00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:55.146
Japan's semiconductor industry
was developing rapidly

00:05:55.146 --> 00:05:57.398
and that many businesses
once belonging to the U.S.

00:05:57.398 --> 00:05:58.733
were snatched away by Japan.

00:05:58.733 --> 00:06:00.526
If they wanted to invest in
an equipment manufacturer,

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:03.529
they should choose the Dutch one,
which was more neutral.

00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:06.157
Therefore,
Intel decided to partner with ASML,

00:06:06.157 --> 00:06:08.910
starting a 30-year-long R&amp;D collaboration.

00:06:08.951 --> 00:06:12.830
Although this project required
a huge amount of time, money, and energy,

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:16.334
the massive investment
would put ASML in an unshakable position

00:06:16.334 --> 00:06:19.420
as a leader in photolithography
30 years later.

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:21.631
Intel hoped ASML could develop

00:06:21.631 --> 00:06:24.425
an EUV equipment
featuring 13.5 nanometer light

00:06:24.425 --> 00:06:27.011
to address smaller process nodes
in the future.

00:06:27.011 --> 00:06:30.306
But just figuring out how to generate
a steady 13.5 nm light source

00:06:30.306 --> 00:06:32.350
was an enormous challenge

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.811
because not a laser source or light bulb

00:06:34.811 --> 00:06:37.814
can directly emit 13.5-nanometer light.

00:06:37.814 --> 00:06:38.898
To emit EUV,

00:06:38.898 --> 00:06:40.983
there must be high-energy plasma.

00:06:41.025 --> 00:06:44.195
The way to produce it is using high-intensity lasers
to hit the target,

00:06:44.195 --> 00:06:48.116
making the target instantly generate the plasma
that emits EUV.

00:06:48.116 --> 00:06:50.368
For the mission of
producing EUV with plasma,

00:06:50.368 --> 00:06:52.495
ASML chose another laser-making company,

00:06:52.495 --> 00:06:54.038
Cymer, to do the job.

00:06:54.038 --> 00:06:57.458
Cymer's task was not simple at all.

00:06:57.458 --> 00:06:59.043
They had to design a process

00:06:59.043 --> 00:07:00.586
that uses carbon dioxide lasers

00:07:00.586 --> 00:07:02.964
to bombard tin metal to generate EUV.

00:07:02.964 --> 00:07:03.756
All in all,

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:04.549
this process

00:07:04.549 --> 00:07:07.593
is certainly much more difficult
than just lighting a bulb.

00:07:07.635 --> 00:07:12.432
First, the tin metal must be turned into
Sn droplet of 30 micrometers in diameter.

00:07:12.432 --> 00:07:15.977
and be shot out by an emitter
at a speed of 300 kilometers per hour

00:07:16.018 --> 00:07:17.687
through a vacuum chamber.

00:07:17.687 --> 00:07:18.771
There's more.

00:07:18.771 --> 00:07:24.068
Each Sn droplet will be hit
by a high-power carbon dioxide laser twice.

00:07:24.068 --> 00:07:26.237
The first hit is for
heating the Sn droplet

00:07:26.237 --> 00:07:27.155
and making it expand.

00:07:27.155 --> 00:07:30.491
The second one
heats it up to 220,000 degrees Celsius,

00:07:30.491 --> 00:07:32.326
turning it into a scorching plasma

00:07:32.326 --> 00:07:35.246
whose temperature is fourty times
then that of the sun’s surface.

00:07:35.246 --> 00:07:36.831
As such, this high-temperature plasma

00:07:36.831 --> 00:07:39.000
will radiate short-wavelength ultraviolet light,

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.335
which is EUV.

00:07:41.335 --> 00:07:44.672
However, this is just a short EUV pulse.

00:07:44.672 --> 00:07:47.300
To generate a steady streak of EUV,

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:50.678
this process needs to be repeated
50,000 times per second.

00:07:50.678 --> 00:07:52.263
In building this bombardment equipment,

00:07:52.263 --> 00:07:55.641
what was just described
was just about 1% of the difficulty.

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:56.267
For example,

00:07:56.267 --> 00:07:57.935
just to create this laser,

00:07:57.935 --> 00:08:00.438
Cymer went to another laser-making company

00:08:00.438 --> 00:08:01.355
—TRUMPF.

00:08:01.355 --> 00:08:03.733
TRUMPF took 10 years to build this laser.

00:08:03.733 --> 00:08:05.568
Eventually, the laser emitter

00:08:05.568 --> 00:08:09.071
consists of
more than 450,000 parts.

00:08:09.071 --> 00:08:10.781
Why make it so complicated?

00:08:10.781 --> 00:08:13.159
It turns out that
after the laser hits the Sn droplet

00:08:13.159 --> 00:08:15.453
the tin will be blasted into debris

00:08:15.453 --> 00:08:17.246
which affects EUV generation.

00:08:17.246 --> 00:08:19.707
Therefore,
the Sn droplet must move fast

00:08:19.707 --> 00:08:22.627
and the laser must be precisely controlled
for perfect timing

00:08:22.668 --> 00:08:25.630
so that the Sn droplet
can keep entering the reaction chamber

00:08:25.630 --> 00:08:27.757
to generate steady EUV light.

00:08:27.757 --> 00:08:28.591
As an aside,

00:08:28.591 --> 00:08:29.675
in addition to tin,

00:08:29.675 --> 00:08:31.677
they have also considered other elements—

00:08:31.677 --> 00:08:34.013
for example, xenon plasma that can emit

00:08:34.013 --> 00:08:37.016
a shorter EUV wavelength
of only 11 nanometers.

00:08:37.016 --> 00:08:39.560
But the EUV intensity emitted by xenon
was insufficient.

00:08:39.602 --> 00:08:41.562
Tin was still the best choice.

00:08:41.562 --> 00:08:42.230
Up to this point,

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:44.607
only half the problems are solved.

00:08:44.607 --> 00:08:46.734
The other half lies in
the lithography process.

00:08:46.734 --> 00:08:47.401
The light

00:08:47.401 --> 00:08:49.946
still needs to pass through
a series of lens sets

00:08:49.946 --> 00:08:51.906
to be cast on chips.

00:08:51.906 --> 00:08:53.366
The biggest challenge here is that

00:08:53.407 --> 00:08:56.369
EUV is easily absorbed by substances.

00:08:56.369 --> 00:08:58.829
To avoid the EUV reacting with air,

00:08:58.829 --> 00:09:00.498
the chamber is pumped into a vacuum.

00:09:00.498 --> 00:09:02.667
This is probably the simplest step of all.

00:09:02.667 --> 00:09:05.628
The real challenge is that
even lenses themselves absorb EUV.

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:06.879
This top-level challenge

00:09:06.879 --> 00:09:09.131
should be dealt with by a top-notch company.

00:09:09.131 --> 00:09:11.467
The leading company in optics from Germany,

00:09:11.467 --> 00:09:14.053
Zeiss, undertook this task.

00:09:14.053 --> 00:09:14.595
First,

00:09:14.595 --> 00:09:17.890
they had to rule out using lens' refraction
as a way to focus the light

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:19.850
because as long as EUV
passes through lenses,

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:22.353
it will be greatly attenuated
by absorption.

00:09:22.353 --> 00:09:26.440
Therefore, optical columns in EUV machines
are an all-reflective system

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:28.234
which is entirely composed of mirrors.

00:09:28.234 --> 00:09:29.860
Although being reflective,

00:09:29.902 --> 00:09:33.030
mirrors can still absorb EUV.

00:09:33.322 --> 00:09:34.865
Eventually, Zeiss coated the mirrors
with two types of layers

00:09:34.865 --> 00:09:37.618
—silicon and molybdenum—

00:09:37.868 --> 00:09:41.080
overlapping each other
dozens to a hundred times,

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:42.331
forming a device called

00:09:42.331 --> 00:09:43.666
the Bragg mirror.

00:09:43.666 --> 00:09:46.627
When light goes through these layers
with alternating refractive indices,

00:09:46.627 --> 00:09:48.004
the Bragg mirror can raise its reflectivity

00:09:48.045 --> 00:09:51.757
by creating more reflection
and constructive interference.

00:09:51.757 --> 00:09:53.009
Now this may sound feasible,

00:09:53.009 --> 00:09:57.096
but it is still
a very very very difficult task.

00:09:57.096 --> 00:09:59.223
This episode is sponsored by
Micro-Epsilon Taiwan.

00:09:59.223 --> 00:10:01.434
Thanks to them, we are able to realize
the pinnacle of human technology.

00:10:01.434 --> 00:10:04.145
To accurately cast the EVU at the target,

00:10:04.145 --> 00:10:06.564
these mirrors must be incredibly smooth.

00:10:06.564 --> 00:10:07.857
How smooth?

00:10:07.857 --> 00:10:09.609
If the mirrors were scaled up to

00:10:09.609 --> 00:10:10.568
let’s say the size of the entire Taiwan island,

00:10:10.568 --> 00:10:15.197
the height deviation on the mirrors
can only be less than 0.01 millimeter.

00:10:15.197 --> 00:10:17.658
That's about the width of a hair.

00:10:17.658 --> 00:10:20.995
The optical precision of this system
is like standing on Earth

00:10:20.995 --> 00:10:23.998
and trying to aim a laser pointer
at a ping pong ball on the Moon.

00:10:23.998 --> 00:10:26.167
Only through this,
the hard-earned EUV light

00:10:26.167 --> 00:10:28.169
can be reflected and focused multiple times

00:10:28.169 --> 00:10:30.296
to pass right through
the nanometer-scale photomask

00:10:30.296 --> 00:10:31.797
and land on the photoresist.

00:10:31.797 --> 00:10:36.052
Intel's Director of Lithography,
Mark Phillips, once said

00:10:36.093 --> 00:10:38.429
EUV is the most precise instrument
ever made by humans.

00:10:38.429 --> 00:10:40.931
It is the ultimate manifestation
of human technology.

00:10:40.931 --> 00:10:41.932
For real-time control and correction

00:10:41.932 --> 00:10:46.520
of these mirror sets, masks,
and the movement of the wafer stages

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:48.814
ultra-high precision sensors are required.

00:10:48.814 --> 00:10:50.816
Devices such as capacitive sensors,

00:10:50.816 --> 00:10:52.401
eddy current inductive sensors,

00:10:52.401 --> 00:10:53.861
or optical ones

00:10:53.903 --> 00:10:55.613
such as confocal chromatic sensors

00:10:55.613 --> 00:10:57.365
and white light interferometer

00:10:57.365 --> 00:10:59.492
—sensors of different measurement mechanisms—

00:10:59.492 --> 00:11:01.744
can cater to different circumstances
and materials,

00:11:01.744 --> 00:11:03.496
achieving sub-nanometer precision.

00:11:03.496 --> 00:11:06.499
They are the core measurement tools
in many precision industries

00:11:06.499 --> 00:11:07.500
such as the semiconductor industry.

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:09.502
The manufacture of
many sensors in EUV machines

00:11:09.502 --> 00:11:12.880
is entrusted to Micro-Epsilon
for customized development.

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:16.258
In addition to
the well-known chromatic confocal technology,

00:11:16.258 --> 00:11:18.844
there are other optical
and non-optical methods

00:11:18.844 --> 00:11:21.430
to monitor such a high-tech beast
like the EUV machine.

00:11:21.430 --> 00:11:23.474
For example,
if a mirror set moves slightly,

00:11:23.474 --> 00:11:26.102
the capacitive sensors
will sense the displacement

00:11:26.102 --> 00:11:27.228
through changes in the dielectric.

00:11:27.269 --> 00:11:28.062
In fact,

00:11:28.062 --> 00:11:31.232
there are nearly
a thousand sensors from Micro-Epsilon

00:11:31.232 --> 00:11:32.191
in an EUV machine.

00:11:32.191 --> 00:11:33.067
Inside it,

00:11:33.067 --> 00:11:35.319
not only the mirror set need correction

00:11:35.319 --> 00:11:36.821
but also the photomask.

00:11:36.821 --> 00:11:38.906
If its position is slightly off,
defects will occur

00:11:38.906 --> 00:11:41.867
when the pattern is being down-sized
and printed on wafers.

00:11:41.909 --> 00:11:43.703
This will subsequently affect yield.

00:11:43.703 --> 00:11:46.163
After all,
current semiconductors are so small

00:11:46.163 --> 00:11:48.958
that even a few layers of atoms
will make a big difference.

00:11:48.958 --> 00:11:50.000
In addition,

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:52.586
the position of the wafer
in relation to the mask also matters.

00:11:52.586 --> 00:11:53.963
Since we can't move

00:11:53.963 --> 00:11:56.132
the laser and optical parts
that have been painstakingly aligned,

00:11:56.132 --> 00:11:59.301
the wafer becomes the one part
that can be moved during exposure.

00:11:59.301 --> 00:12:00.928
The dual-stage system of the EUV machine

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:02.972
can move two wafers simultaneously.

00:12:02.972 --> 00:12:04.473
One undergoes exposure

00:12:04.473 --> 00:12:07.727
while the other is measured for precision
at the same time.

00:12:07.727 --> 00:12:10.146
The stages move at high speed
with six degrees of freedom.

00:12:10.146 --> 00:12:11.564
The sensors used here

00:12:11.564 --> 00:12:14.567
are eddy current sensors
with nanometer-level precision.

00:12:14.567 --> 00:12:16.861
Because Micro-Epsilon Group
values the Taiwan market,

00:12:16.902 --> 00:12:19.113
in 2022,
it established a subsidiary in Taiwan

00:12:19.113 --> 00:12:20.030
—Micro-Epsilon Taiwan—

00:12:20.030 --> 00:12:22.992
to provide more localized service
for manufacturers in Taiwan.

00:12:22.992 --> 00:12:24.535
From standard catalog items

00:12:24.535 --> 00:12:26.787
to customized products
matching clients' needs,

00:12:26.787 --> 00:12:30.249
it provides Taiwan customers with
sensor solutions of ultra-high precision.

00:12:30.249 --> 00:12:32.334
Through experience in collaborating
with top manufacturers

00:12:32.334 --> 00:12:33.919
such as ASML and Zeiss,

00:12:33.919 --> 00:12:36.297
it can offer assistance in upgrading
Taiwan's semiconductor equipment.

00:12:36.297 --> 00:12:38.007
In measuring displacement
and positioning components

00:12:38.007 --> 00:12:40.009
of equipment in every supply chain,

00:12:40.009 --> 00:12:42.136
Micro-Epsilon sensors
can be seen everywhere

00:12:42.136 --> 00:12:43.554
such as in Lapping machine

00:12:43.554 --> 00:12:44.847
temporary bonding equipment,

00:12:44.847 --> 00:12:45.806
Dispensing machine,

00:12:45.806 --> 00:12:47.224
high-precision die bonders,

00:12:47.224 --> 00:12:49.268
wafer metrology machine and so on.

00:12:49.310 --> 00:12:50.144
As of now,

00:12:50.144 --> 00:12:51.353
I believe everyone understands that,

00:12:51.353 --> 00:12:52.772
in the rise of the semiconductor industry,

00:12:52.772 --> 00:12:55.900
why chip design, chip manufacturing,
and equipment manufacturing

00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:59.278
are so closely related and highly symbiotic.

00:12:59.278 --> 00:13:00.988
Let's go back to the birth of
the EUV machine.

00:13:00.988 --> 00:13:02.031
History tells us

00:13:02.031 --> 00:13:03.491
Zeiss and Cymer

00:13:03.491 --> 00:13:05.659
both fulfilled their missions.

00:13:05.659 --> 00:13:07.286
ASML has been dominating the market

00:13:07.286 --> 00:13:12.333
ever since it built the first prototype of
an EUV machine in 2006.

00:13:12.333 --> 00:13:15.795
Can there be another company
matching ASML?

00:13:16.462 --> 00:13:17.755
Probably not.

00:13:17.755 --> 00:13:19.006
A company of this scale

00:13:19.006 --> 00:13:21.258
with countless innovations
and integrations of technology

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:22.968
requires decades of development

00:13:22.968 --> 00:13:26.096
and a billion-dollar investment to build.

00:13:26.096 --> 00:13:26.931
On the flip side,

00:13:26.931 --> 00:13:29.725
should we worry about
ASML monopolizing the market?

00:13:30.434 --> 00:13:32.436
Well, its monopoly is already a fact,

00:13:32.436 --> 00:13:35.397
but ASML is not alone.

00:13:35.606 --> 00:13:37.608
As you can see
in the previous introduction,

00:13:37.608 --> 00:13:38.984
the birth of EUV machines

00:13:38.984 --> 00:13:42.655
was based on the joint efforts of
many companies and suppliers,

00:13:42.655 --> 00:13:44.949
especially the U.S. company Cymer,

00:13:44.949 --> 00:13:46.367
who built the tin droplet
bombardment equipment

00:13:46.367 --> 00:13:49.787
and the German company Zeiss,
who made the reflective mirrors.

00:13:49.787 --> 00:13:52.873
Only 15% of the components
are produced by ASML itself.

00:13:53.332 --> 00:13:56.126
Its key technology lies in
assembling these parts

00:13:56.168 --> 00:13:59.129
into a working EUV machine.

00:13:59.630 --> 00:14:01.882
Although ASML later acquired Cymer

00:14:01.882 --> 00:14:03.509
and invested in Zeiss,

00:14:03.509 --> 00:14:06.262
the EUV machine cannot be developed by

00:14:06.262 --> 00:14:08.013
a single company or country alone.

00:14:08.013 --> 00:14:09.598
It represents cutting-edge technology

00:14:09.598 --> 00:14:12.268
created through collaboration
among many countries and companies.

00:14:12.268 --> 00:14:14.019
Right now,

00:14:14.019 --> 00:14:16.897
ASML has already aimed at
sub-2nm processes.

00:14:16.897 --> 00:14:18.649
Last year, it launched the latest

00:14:18.649 --> 00:14:20.734
high numerical aperture EUV machine

00:14:20.734 --> 00:14:23.028
and sold the first one to Intel.

00:14:23.028 --> 00:14:25.990
Rumor has it that TSMC has already acquired an EUV machine.

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:27.408
But it's still considered classified information.

00:14:27.408 --> 00:14:29.827
We'll just have to wait and see on this one.

00:14:29.869 --> 00:14:31.537
This is the end of this episode.

00:14:31.537 --> 00:14:33.873
Next time when you see an ASML truck,

00:14:33.873 --> 00:14:35.082
remember to drive carefully.

00:14:35.082 --> 00:14:36.917
You don't want to pay for a car accident like this.

00:14:36.917 --> 00:14:37.877
See you next time.

